  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ミジンコウキクサ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%9F%E3%82%B8%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B3%E3%82%A6%E3%82%AD%E3%82%AF%E3%82%B5",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 108297296,
      "text": "ミジンコウキクサ はウキクサ亜科ミジンコウキクサ属に属する水生植物の1種であり、水面に浮遊して生育する。からだは極めて微小 であり、根を欠く。ミジンコウキクサ属の中でも小型の種であり、世界最小の種子植物ともされる。日本を含むアジアに広く分布しており、タイなどでは食用とされることもある。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Wolffia globosa」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolffia_globosa",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1365198247,
      "text": "Wolffia globosa is a species of flowering plant known by the common names Asian watermeal and duckweed. It is native to Asia and is found in parts of the Americas and Africa, where it is an introduced species. It grows in mats on the surface of calm, freshwater bodies, such as ponds, lakes, and marshes. It is a very tiny, oval-shaped plant with no leaves, stems, or roots. The body of the plant, a transparent green frond, is less than a millimeter wide. In one human experiment, processed W. globosa was reported to provide dietary protein and vitamin B12."
    }
  },
